--TEST--
Core\Path::extension answers null for a name whose only dot is its first byte or its last, where pathinfo reads the whole dotfile name as an extension and an empty string as one — which is what makes withExtension an inverse rather than a rename
--ORACLE-DIVERGES--
`pathinfo` splits a name at its last dot and asks nothing else, so `.gitignore` decomposes to the filename `''` and the extension `'gitignore'`, `report.` to `report` and `''`, and `..` to `.` and `''`. `Core\Path` declines all three (`path.rs:26`, which is the one home of why): a leading dot does not open an extension, because a dotfile's whole name *is* its name; a trailing dot does not either, because an empty extension is an absence and ADR 0063 R5 makes `null` the one spelling of absence. The consequence is the reason it is worth diverging for — `withExtension` takes exactly what `extension` answered, so the two are inverses on every name, whereas `pathinfo`'s reading recomposes `.gitignore` with a new extension into `.txt`, which is a *different file* and not a renamed one. PHP has no member to compare `withExtension` against at all; the rows below spell out what the recomposition idiom built on `pathinfo`'s keys would have produced beside what this answers. Note that the divergence is about which dot, not about dots: a dotfile that also carries an extension is `.hidden.txt` and both readers answer `txt`, which the last rows pin here so the rule is not read as "a dotfile has no extension".
--FILE--
<?nvs
// The agreeing decomposition is
// `path-decomposition-matches-pathinfos-four-keys-over-a-name-that-has-an-extension`'s,
// and `basename`'s own suffix argument is
// `path-basename-matches-basename-including-its-suffix-argument`'s — where
// PHP happens to agree on a dotfile for a different reason, because it refuses
// a suffix that is the whole name. This case is `extension` itself.
final class Show {
    public static function of(string $path): string {
        return "[" . Core\Path::basename($path, {withoutExtension: true})
            . "][" . (Core\Path::extension($path) ?? "<null>") . "]";
    }
}

// A leading dot. PHP answers the filename `''` and the extension `gitignore`
// for the first two, and there is no reading of that under which the name has
// been decomposed into anything usable.
echo Show::of(".gitignore"), "\n";
echo Show::of("/etc/.env"), "\n";
echo Show::of(".a"), "\n";

// A trailing dot. PHP answers an extension that is present and empty, which is
// the one shape ADR 0063 R5 has no spelling for.
echo Show::of("report."), "\n";
echo Show::of("/var/log/app."), "\n";

// `.` and `..` are the two names that are nothing but dots, and PHP reads the
// second as the name `.` carrying an empty extension. Both are ordinary
// components to this reader, resolved by `normalize` alone and by nothing here.
echo Show::of("."), "\n";
echo Show::of(".."), "\n";
echo Show::of("/a/b/.."), "\n";

// Which is what makes the pair invertible: `withExtension` takes what
// `extension` answered, so putting back what was read is the identity, and
// giving a dotfile an extension keeps the dotfile's name instead of consuming
// it. The idiom over `pathinfo`'s keys answers `.txt` for the first of these.
// The second is what the rule costs where it costs anything: `report.`'s
// trailing dot is a byte of its name, so it is still there under the new
// extension and the answer has two dots in it — a name that round-trips,
// where PHP's `report.txt` has quietly dropped a byte the caller wrote.
echo Core\Path::withExtension(".gitignore", "txt"), "\n";
echo Core\Path::withExtension("report.", "txt"), "\n";
echo Core\Path::withExtension(".gitignore", null), "\n";
echo Core\Path::withExtension("report.txt", null), "\n";

// And the rule is about which dot, not about dots: a dotfile that carries an
// extension has one, and both readers say `txt`.
echo Show::of(".hidden.txt"), "\n";
echo Show::of("/etc/.config.json"), "\n";
--EXPECT--
[.gitignore][<null>]
[.env][<null>]
[.a][<null>]
[report.][<null>]
[app.][<null>]
[.][<null>]
[..][<null>]
[..][<null>]
.gitignore.txt
report..txt
.gitignore
report
[.hidden][txt]
[.config][json]
